# Competency question: "What hypothesis, null distribution, and tail/sidedness produced
# this p-value, and was it adjusted for multiplicity?" Exercises the inference-and-results
# spine: a math:PValue names the math:NullHypothesis it is computed under
# (math:nullHypothesis), the math:Distribution it is measured against (math:nullDistribution,
# here a math:SamplingDistribution), and its math:Sidedness (math:alternativeSidedness) —
# the frame that makes P(T ≥ t | H0) unambiguous. Its multiplicity handling is
# LEFT-JOINed (OPTIONAL): a p-value MAY name the adjustment applied to it
# (math:pValueAdjustment) and the family of comparisons it is controlled over
# (math:multipleComparisonFamily), so "was it adjusted for multiplicity, and over what
# family" is answerable rather than implicit. The adjustment/family columns are
# legitimately unbound for an unadjusted p-value (BindingOptional); over the fixture they
# ARE bound, so the answer states the p-value's null, distribution, sidedness, AND its
# Benjamini–Hochberg adjustment over its comparison family. Scoped to the fixture
# namespace so the answer is exactly the single framing row.
PREFIX math:  <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/math/>

SELECT ?pvalue ?nullHypothesis ?nullDistribution ?sidedness ?adjustment ?family WHERE {
    ?pvalue a math:PValue ;
            math:nullHypothesis ?nullHypothesis ;
            math:nullDistribution ?nullDistribution ;
            math:alternativeSidedness ?sidedness .
    OPTIONAL { ?pvalue math:pValueAdjustment ?adjustment . }
    OPTIONAL { ?pvalue math:multipleComparisonFamily ?family . }
    FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?pvalue), "http://example.org/math/"))
}
ORDER BY ?pvalue ?nullHypothesis
